    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Background: \r\nThe 2013 Pooled Cohort Equations (PCEs) play a central role in cardiovascular disease (CVD) prevention guidelines.\r\n\r\nObjective:\r\nTo test two hypotheses for improving PCE calibration: (i) updating the PCEs by incorporating newer cohort data; and (ii) additionally employing novel statistical methods to reduce over-fitting and overcome the proportional hazards assumption. \r\n\r\nDesign:\r\nDerivation and validation of risk equations.\r\n\r\nSetting: \r\nPopulation-based.\r\n\r\nParticipants: \r\nN = 26,689 adults 40-79 years old without prior CVD, from six cohorts.\r\n\r\nMeasurements:\r\nNonfatal myocardial infarction or coronary heart disease death, or fatal or nonfatal stroke.\r\n\r\nResults:\r\nUpdating the PCEs by incorporating newer cohorts and omitting the oldest cohort reduced risk estimates, but did not resolve limited calibration (chi-squared difference between observed and expected CVD rates = 16.9 to 28.8 among race/sex subgroups; P-value < 0.05 for significant differences). Black adult risk estimates also continued to span extreme values suggestive of over-fitting. Re-deriving the PCEs using elastic net regularization and logistic regression adjusted for censoring improved calibration (chi-squared = 8.3 to 17.3, P = 0.17 to 0.92 among subgroups) and discrimination (increasing C-statistics by 0.02 to 0.04 to range 0.73 [Black men] to 0.81 [Black women]).\r\n\r\nConclusion: \r\nUpdating the 2013 PCEs with data from modern cohorts was insufficient to improve calibration; additional measures to reduce over-fitting particularly among African-Americans, and to avoid the proportional hazards assumption, improved calibration further. Our findings also indicate that risk equations will generally become outdated over time, requiring routine updating in the future.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
